AB  - As part of the BNL-JLab-CEA Electron Ion Collider (EIC) collaboration, the design of a 2 T, 2.8 m bore diameter, 3.8 m long conduction cooled superconducting detector magnet design is completed. Such a magnet will be employed at the interaction region of ePIC for physics experiments. The magnet is a passive shielded solenoidal magnet system consisting of three coils wound with a specially designed conductor using niobium-titanium (NbTi) Rutherford-type cable in a copper-stabilized channel. This article describes the risk analysis as the part of failure modes and effects analysis (FMEA) that was carried out as a team to identify their various failure modes and risks associated with the magnet system. This FMEA is intended to become the content of the designed document as an integral part of the engineering assessment and the statement of work for the potential vendors toward design and built.
